<h1>Networking &amp; HTTP</h1>
<p>Multiplayer sessions use a host-authoritative network contract for spawning, ownership, and message validation. HTTP/HTTPS is a separate asynchronous service for REST backends; it needs no multiplayer contract.</p>

<h2>Network contract</h2>
<p>Declare a contract asset in the project:</p>
<pre><code>"network_contract": "asset://network/arena_contract"</code></pre>
<p>The <code>NetworkContract</code> asset declares replicated prefab keys, field writers, message names and directions, size and rate limits, and payload schemas. The runtime includes the contract and referenced schemas in a compatibility hash; incompatible packets are rejected during session setup.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Values</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Senders / writers</td><td><code>server</code>, <code>owner</code>, <code>all</code></td><td>Who may send a message or write a replicated field; only the server may spawn</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Disconnect</td><td><code>despawn</code>, <code>return_to_server</code>, <code>transfer_by_game_policy</code></td><td>What happens to that player’s entities</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Message targets</td><td><code>owned_entity</code>, <code>entity</code>, <code>none</code></td><td>What the message is addressed to</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Session flow</h2>
<pre><code>local NetworkSession = require("demi.network.session")

-- Host:
NetworkSession.host(39420)                       -- boolean
NetworkSession.start_session({
    scene_id = "scene://game",
    seed     = 42,
})

-- Client:
NetworkSession.connect("127.0.0.1", 39420)       -- boolean: attempt started</code></pre>
<p><code>start_session(metadata)</code> sends metadata from the host; it does not load the named scene or seed the simulation. Check <code>process_events()</code> for connection and session events.</p>
<p>Poll the session each frame:</p>
<pre><code>function Game:on_update(dt)
    local update = NetworkSession.process_events()
    for _, e in ipairs(update.events) do
        print(e.name, e.sender_id)
    end
end</code></pre>
<p><code>process_events()</code> returns an update table with <code>connected</code>, <code>disconnected</code>, <code>session_started</code>, <code>session</code>, <code>messages</code>, and <code>events</code>. The events array contains accepted gameplay and prediction events; invalid contract traffic is rejected before delivery. On an owning peer, call <code>update_entity(network_id, dt)</code> to publish contract-declared replicated fields at the configured interval.</p>
<p>Diagnostics expose the lifecycle phase, including <code>closed</code>, <code>connected</code>, <code>authenticated</code>, <code>ready</code>, and <code>active</code>:</p>
<pre><code>local d = NetworkSession.diagnostics()
-- d.phase, d.session_epoch, d.contract_hash, d.sent_messages, d.rejected_messages

print(NetworkSession.is_connected())   -- boolean
print(NetworkSession.is_host())        -- boolean
print(NetworkSession.sender_id())      -- string peer ID
print(NetworkSession.current_session())-- metadata table or nil</code></pre>

<h2>Authority and spawning</h2>
<pre><code>-- Host only:
local id = NetworkSession.spawn("player", entity_id, owner_id) -- contract prefab key
NetworkSession.transfer(network_id, new_owner)
NetworkSession.despawn(network_id)

if NetworkSession.has_authority(network_id) then
    -- safe to apply local gameplay for this entity
end

local owner = NetworkSession.owner(network_id)
local nid = NetworkSession.network_id_for_owner(owner_id)
NetworkSession.bind_local_entity(network_id, entity_id)</code></pre>
<p><code>spawn</code> accepts a key in <code>replicated_prefabs</code> and an existing local entity ID; it returns a string network ID or <code>nil</code>. <code>transfer</code>, <code>despawn</code>, and <code>bind_local_entity</code> return booleans. Binding is permitted only for the owning peer. <code>has_authority</code> tests ownership; individual field and message rules still apply. Late joiners receive retained spawn state.</p>

<h2>Messages</h2>
<pre><code>local ok = NetworkSession.send("player_join", nil, { color = { 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, 1.0 } })
-- second argument is a network entity ID when the contract requires a target</code></pre>
<p>Only contract-declared message names are accepted. A payload must match its declared schema, finite-number rules, and size limits. The validation pipeline checks header, protocol, contract hash, epoch, sequence, size, structure, schema, sender, destination, ownership generation, and rate before delivery.</p>

<h2>Prediction and reconciliation</h2>
<p>Optional, for responsive movement on a locally owned client entity:</p>
<pre><code>NetworkSession.configure({
    input_queue_capacity = 64,
    input_future_window = 8,
    input_head_of_line_timeout = 0.5,
    input_max_per_tick = 8,
    snapshot_buffer = 32,
    interpolation_delay = 0.1,
    extrapolation_limit = 0.25,
    prediction_history_limit = 128,
    prediction_visual_decay = 12.0,
})

local owned_id = NetworkSession.network_id_for_owner(NetworkSession.sender_id())
NetworkSession.enable_prediction({
    network_id = owned_id,
    input_message = "move_input", -- declared owner-to-server owned_entity message
    state = { x = 0, y = 0 },
    apply = function(state, input)
        return {
            x = state.x + input.dx * input.dt,
            y = state.y + input.dy * input.dt,
        }
    end,
})</code></pre>
<p>The <code>apply</code> callback returns a deterministic next controller state. The host consumes inputs and publishes authoritative snapshots.</p>
<p>Related calls and what they return:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.publish_snapshot(nid, state)</code></td><td>Host publishes authoritative state; returns a boolean.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.predict_input(nid, input)</code></td><td>Applies and sends one client input; returns its sequence number or <code>nil</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.take_inputs(nid)</code></td><td>Host receives ordered accepted input tables, each with <code>seq</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.prediction_state(nid)</code></td><td>Current predicted state table, or <code>nil</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.prediction_visual_offset(nid)</code></td><td>Render-only offset table keyed by state field, or <code>nil</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.remote_state(nid)</code></td><td>Interpolated remote state table, or <code>nil</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.prediction_diagnostics()</code></td><td>Per-channel counters under <code>channels</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NetworkSession.reset_prediction(nid, state)</code></td><td>Rebases to a supplied state; returns a boolean.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>On the host, historical 2D queries use recorded collision circles for lag-compensated hits:</p>
<pre><code>NetworkSession.configure({
    query_history_capacity = 64,
    query_history_max_entities = 128,
    query_history_rewind_ticks = 8,
})
NetworkSession.record_query_snapshot(server_tick, {
    { entity_id = nid, x = px, y = py, radius = 0.5 },
})
local hit = NetworkSession.historical_raycast(
    requested_tick, origin_x, origin_y, direction_x, direction_y, max_distance
) -- 2D circle hit table or nil</code></pre>

<h2>Low-level transport</h2>
<pre><code>local Network = require("demi.network")

print(Network.available())          -- true when transport is compiled in
Network.host(port)                   -- ENet
Network.host_dtls(port, cert_path, key_path) -- DTLS
Network.connect(host, port)
Network.send(bytes, reliable, peer_id, channel) -- raw bytes payload
local events = Network.events()      -- connected, disconnected, and message events</code></pre>
<p>Use this layer for custom protocols. Most games want <code>demi.network.session</code> instead.</p>

<h2>TLS</h2>
<pre><code>local TlsServer = require("demi.network.tls.server")
local TlsClient = require("demi.network.tls.client")

TlsServer.listen(port, cert_path, key_path)
local ok = TlsClient.connect(host, port, trusted_cert_path, server_name)
if not ok then print(TlsClient.error()) end

TlsClient.send(payload)
local events = TlsClient.events()    -- receive queue
print(TlsClient.is_connected())      -- boolean</code></pre>
<p>The TLS client verifies the certificate chain against the supplied trust file and verifies the server name. <code>connect</code> starts the connection; poll <code>events()</code> or <code>is_connected()</code> for the result. <code>demi.network.crypto</code> provides <code>random_token()</code>, <code>password_hash(password, salt[, iterations])</code>, and <code>secure_equals(a, b)</code>. Tokens are hex strings.</p>

<h2>HTTP / HTTPS</h2>
<pre><code>local Http = require("demi.network.http")

local request, err = Http.post("https://api.example.com/login", {
    json = { user = "hero" },          -- JSON body + Content-Type
    headers = { ["X-Trace"] = "1" },
    timeout_ms = 10000,
    connect_timeout_ms = 2000,
    max_response_bytes = 1024 * 1024,
})
if not request then
    print("could not start request:", err)
    return
end</code></pre>
<p><code>Http.get</code>, <code>Http.post</code>, and <code>Http.request</code> (for PUT/PATCH/DELETE/HEAD) return a request handle immediately, or <code>nil, error</code> when the call could not be started. Poll the handle:</p>
<pre><code>function Game:on_update()
    if not request:done() then return end

    local response = request:response()
    if response.ok then
        -- status 200–299
        print(response.status)
        print(response.body)               -- raw string
        local data = response.json         -- parsed table, or nil + response.json_error
        if data then print(data.user) end
    else
        print(response.status, response.error, response.error_code)
    end
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>request:done()</code> — boolean, true when finished (success or failure).</li>
  <li><code>request:response()</code> — response table while done, <code>nil</code> while pending.</li>
  <li><code>request:cancel()</code> — abort; garbage collection also cancels.</li>
  <li>Response fields: <code>ok</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>body</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>error_code</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>json_error</code>, <code>headers</code> (lowercase names, array values).</li>
  <li>Use either <code>json</code> or <code>body</code> on the request, not both.</li>
  <li>Redirects come back as 3xx — follow them yourself if you want to.</li>
  <li>Android projects with an explicit build block need <code>android.permission.INTERNET</code> in <code>build.android.permissions</code> when they use network services. Projects without an authored build block receive the engine's default permission.</li>
</ul>

